\section{Items already in Zotero}{

Before importing, the selected \strong{library} (all of it, not only the selected
collection) is searched for items whose Extra holds an OpenAlex ID, in any
of the forms in use: \code{OpenAlex: W...} (written by this package),
\verb{OpenAlex: https://openalex.org/W...} (Zotero's OpenAlex translators),
\code{openalex.work_id: W...} and \verb{OpenAlex Work ID: W...} (two Zotero plugins).
Items whose OpenAlex ID is not found there (or that have none) are imported.
Items already there are handled by \code{existing}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"skip"} (default): left untouched and not imported.
\item \code{"update"}: the existing Zotero item gets the new metadata; fields the
new metadata does not have keep their old values.
\item \code{"overwrite"}: all fields of the existing Zotero item are replaced by the
new metadata; fields it does not have are emptied.
}

With \code{"update"} and \code{"overwrite"} the existing item keeps its item key,
child notes and attachments, tags, collections and related items.
Technically, the items are imported with Zotero's CSL JSON importer, their
fields are copied onto the existing items, and the imported copies are
deleted again.
}

\section{Tags}{

CSL JSON has no tags, so by default items arrive without tags, and existing
items keep theirs. With \code{tags} (e.g. the \code{zotero_tags.parquet} written by
\code{\link[=zotero_tags]{zotero_tags()}}), tags are written by OpenAlex ID, following \code{existing}:
\itemize{
\item new items get the tags of their OpenAlex ID;
\item \code{"skip"}: existing items are left untouched, tags included;
\item \code{"update"}: tags missing on the existing item are added, its own are kept;
\item \code{"overwrite"}: the existing item's tags are replaced by those in \code{tags},
and removed if \code{tags} has none for its OpenAlex ID.
}

Rows of several libraries are merged; a tag keeps its type (manual or
automatic).
}

\section{Collection}{

With \code{collection}, e.g. \code{c("my_project", "corpus_a")}, the works are also
put into that collection, nested from the top level of the selected
library (\code{my_project} > \code{corpus_a}). Missing collections are created,
existing ones of the same name reused, so a re-export goes into the same
collection. Works already in the library join the collection too, with
every value of \code{existing}: collections are independent of the item, and
one work can be in several. Other collections of an item are kept.

New items also land in whatever is selected in Zotero, so select the
library itself (not a collection) to have them only in \code{collection}.
Deleting the collection in Zotero with its items removes an accidental
export again; items that were already in the library before stay in their
other collections.
}

\section{Write access}{

\code{"update"}, \code{"overwrite"}, \code{tags} and \code{collection} write through Zotero's
local API,
which needs Zotero 10 or later and a key. If \code{api_key} is empty, Zotero shows a dialog asking to allow access for
"openalexConvert": choose \strong{Always Allow} (a key allowed once is only good
for a single write). The key is kept for the rest of the R session.

Progress is reported via the \pkg{progressr} package.
}

\section{Speed}{

Zotero gets slower as the library grows. Measured with Zotero 10.0.6 on a
Mac, importing 5000 works in files of 1000 into a library starting at about
440 items:\tabular{rrrr}{
   file \tab items in library before \tab time \tab works/s \cr
   1 \tab 440 \tab 57 s \tab 17.5 \cr
   2 \tab 1440 \tab 89 s \tab 11.2 \cr
   3 \tab 2440 \tab 121 s \tab 8.3 \cr
   4 \tab 3440 \tab 145 s \tab 6.9 \cr
   5 \tab 4440 \tab 163 s \tab 6.1 \cr
}


Zotero's memory use grew to 3.4 GB during that run. For 100000 works, plan
for several hours to more than a day, and consider restarting Zotero every
few files.

The search for items already in the library (run once per call, for every
value of \code{existing}) took 0.2 s for 369 IDs and 6.6 s for 5369; it grows
faster than the library, so expect tens of minutes for 100000. Re-running
all 5000 works with \code{existing = "skip"} took 4.7 s, mostly that search.
\code{\link[=corpus_to_csljson]{corpus_to_csljson()}} itself takes about 1 ms per work.
}

